.TH CLAWSEC 1 "November 2026" "ClawSec 4.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clawsec \- encrypted netcat with a password-authenticated key exchange (SPAKE2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clawsec
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-K ]
.RB [ \-u ]
.RB [ \-4 | \-6 ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.B \-k
.I password
.RB [ \-L
.IR host:port ]
.RB [ \-\-obfs
.IR http | tls ]
.RB [ \-\-fingerprint
.IR chrome | firefox | safari ]
.RB [ \-\-tofu ]
.RB [ \-\-pq ]
.RB [ \-\-ech ]
.RB [ \-\-mux ]
.RB [ \-\-fallback
.IR host:port ]
.RB [ \-\-pad ]
.RB [ \-\-jitter
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-P ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR secs ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR program ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RI [ hostname ]
.RI [ port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clawsec
is an encrypted netcat alternative evolved from Cryptcat. Sessions are
established with SPAKE2, a password-authenticated key exchange (RFC 9382),
and protected with AES-256-GCM.
.PP
An eavesdropper or man-in-the-middle cannot test password guesses offline:
every guess costs a connection attempt. Keys are ephemeral, so even if the
password is later compromised, previously recorded sessions cannot be
decrypted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-k " password"
Session password, shared by both ends and verified by the SPAKE2 handshake.
Visible to other local users in the process list; prefer \fB\-\-key\-file\fR
or \fBCLAWSEC_PASSWORD\fR. Use a random password (\fBopenssl rand \-base64 24\fR).
.TP
.B \-l
Listen mode. Wait for inbound connections.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Local port number for listen mode.
.TP
.B \-K
Keep-open mode. In listen mode, accept multiple clients by forking
a new process per connection. Each child performs its own handshake.
See \fB\-\-max\-clients\fR, \fB\-\-max\-per\-ip\fR and
\fB\-\-auth\-fail\-limit\fR. TCP only.
.TP
.BI \-L " host:port"
Port forwarding mode. After establishing the encrypted tunnel,
forward all decrypted traffic to the specified host:port.
Replaces \fBssh \-L\fR without requiring SSH. Supports IPv6
with bracket notation: \fB[::1]:8080\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-obfs " mode"
Traffic obfuscation. Supported modes:
.RS
.TP
.B http
Wrap encrypted frames in HTTP POST requests with realistic headers
to evade shallow DPI. Traffic pattern may still be detectable by
advanced statistical analysis.
.TP
.B tls
Wrap the entire connection in a real TLS 1.3 session with an
auto-generated EC certificate and randomized CDN-like SNI hostname.
Passive observers see a TLS 1.3 connection; the server uses a
self-signed certificate, which anyone who connects can see.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-pad
Pad every encrypted record to a multiple of 1400 bytes with random
bytes, in every mode (stream, \fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-mux\fR, \fB\-\-socks\fR,
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-tun\fR, \fB\-\-tun\-udp\fR, file transfer);
stream-like modes send records of exactly 1400 bytes. Hides message sizes,
not timing or volume. Negotiated in the handshake: both ends must use
\fB\-\-pad\fR (ClawSec 4.2+), otherwise the connection fails with an
option mismatch. With \fB\-\-tun\fR the interface MTU is 1392.
.TP
.BI \-\-jitter " ms"
Add a random delay of 0 to \fIms\fR milliseconds between each
packet sent. Defeats timing correlation attacks used by advanced
DPI and traffic analysis systems.
.TP
.B \-\-ech
Encrypted Client Hello. Adds a GREASE ECH extension to the TLS
ClientHello, making the SNI invisible to DPI. Automatically enables
TLS mode (\fB\-\-obfs tls\fR) if not already set. The server does
not need \fB\-\-ech\fR; it accepts the extension transparently.
.TP
.B \-\-mux
Stream multiplexer. Allows multiple logical connections over a
single encrypted tunnel. In server mode, requires \fB\-L host:port\fR
to specify the target. In client mode, requires \fB\-p [host:]port\fR to
specify the local listening port (default host 127.0.0.1). Up to 255
concurrent streams (63 with a ClawSec 4.0 peer) with per-stream flow
control and half-close; stream IDs are reused.
.TP
.BI \-\-fallback " host:port"
After the TLS handshake, a client that does not send the ClawSec knock
(browser, probe, scanner) is transparently proxied to the service at
\fIhost:port\fR; only clients that send the knock proceed to the
encrypted tunnel. Probers still see ClawSec's self-signed certificate. Automatically enables
TLS mode. Both client and server must use \fB\-\-fallback\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-tofu
Trust On First Use. The server maintains a persistent Ed25519 identity
keypair in \fB~/.clawsec/identity\fR. During the handshake, the server
signs the handshake transcript. The client verifies the signature and checks
\fB~/.clawsec/known_hosts\fR. If the server identity changes, the
connection is aborted with a warning (like SSH). Both client and server
must use \fB\-\-tofu\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-pq
Post-quantum hybrid key exchange: an ML-KEM-768 shared secret is mixed
into the SPAKE2 session key, so recorded sessions stay confidential
against a future quantum adversary (the password check itself is not
post-quantum). Requires OpenSSL >= 3.5.
Both client and server must use \fB\-\-pq\fR.
Can be combined with \fB\-\-tofu\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-tun " ip/mask"
TUN VPN mode. Create a virtual network interface and relay encrypted
IP packets through the tunnel. Assigns the given IP address and subnet
mask to the TUN device. Requires root/sudo. Example: \fB\-\-tun 10.0.0.1/24\fR.
Supports macOS (utun) and Linux (/dev/net/tun). A client may use
\fB\-\-tun auto\fR to get its addresses from a ClawSec 4.2+ server, or ask
for a specific one. With \fB\-K\fR the server is a hub for many clients: one
device, one process per client, addresses from the \fB\-\-tun\fR subnet
(/8\-/30), routing by destination and a source-address check per client.
.TP
.BI \-\-tun6 " ip6/prefix"
Server: IPv6 inside the tunnel (prefix 64\-120, e.g. \fBfd00:c1a5::1/64\fR).
Clients get the address with the same host number as their IPv4 address.
With \fB\-\-masquerade\fR IPv6 is NATed too; a client's
\fB\-\-default\-route\fR adds ::/1 and 8000::/1 through the tunnel.
.TP
.B \-\-tun\-udp
Use a UDP data channel for VPN traffic instead of TCP. After the TCP
handshake (ECDHE key exchange), a dedicated UDP socket is opened for
VPN data with per-packet AES-256-GCM encryption. Eliminates TCP-over-TCP
meltdown and reduces latency. Falls back to TCP on failure.
Both sides must use \fB\-\-tun\-udp\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-masquerade
Enable NAT/masquerade on the server side. Allows VPN clients to access
the internet through the server as an exit node. Uses iptables/nftables
on Linux, pf on macOS. Use with \fB\-\-tun\fR on the server.
.TP
.B \-\-default\-route
Full tunnel mode (client-side). Routes ALL traffic through the VPN,
similar to WireGuard \fBAllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0\fR. Saves and restores
original routes on disconnect (including Ctrl+C/kill). Server must
have \fB\-\-masquerade\fR enabled.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan " range"
Port scanner. Scan target host for open ports with randomized order.
Range syntax: \fB1-1024\fR, \fB80\fR, \fBall\fR (1-65535).
Does not require encryption password.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan\-rate " pps"
SYN scan speed in probes per second (default 1000, max 100000). As root on
Linux the SYN scan sends probes at this rate while collecting replies,
probes unanswered ports once more and reports them as filtered; replies
are matched by a keyed initial sequence number. IPv4 and IPv6.
.TP
.B \-b
Banner grabbing. During port scan, detect service versions on open ports.
.TP
.BI \-\-socks " [host:]port"
SOCKS5 proxy through encrypted tunnel. Client opens a local SOCKS5
listener on \fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR (default host 127.0.0.1, or ::1 with
\fB\-6\fR); server proxies outbound connections. With a ClawSec 4.1+
server every SOCKS connection is its own multiplexed stream, so
connections run concurrently; with an older server they are handled one at
a time. The proxy has no authentication: bind it to a non-loopback address
only deliberately.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " file"
Send a file through encrypted tunnel with SHA-256 verification,
automatic resume, and progress bar.
.TP
.BI \-\-recv " dir"
Receive a file into \fIdir\fR through encrypted tunnel with
SHA-256 verification and path traversal protection.
.TP
.BI \-R " host:port"
Reverse tunnel, SSH-like \fB\-R\fR. On the server, \fIhost:port\fR is the
address to listen on (127.0.0.1 for local only, 0.0.0.0 to expose
publicly); incoming connections are tunneled to the client, where
\fIhost:port\fR is the target to connect to.
.TP
.B \-\-persistent
Auto-reconnect with exponential backoff (1s\(en60s, \(+-25% jitter).
Turns any tunnel into a stable persistent channel.
.TP
.BI \-\-fingerprint " profile"
Shape the TLS ClientHello to match a real browser. Supported profiles:
\fBchrome\fR (Chrome 124+), \fBfirefox\fR (Firefox 125+),
\fBsafari\fR (Safari 17+). Sets cipher suites, supported groups,
signature algorithms, ALPN, and browser-specific extensions (ALPS
for Chrome). Automatically enables TLS mode.
.TP
.B \-z
Compress data with zlib (deflate) before encryption. Reduces
bandwidth for text and compressible files. Both sides must use \fB\-z\fR.
.TP
.B \-P
Show a transfer progress bar on stderr with speed and bytes
transferred. Built-in alternative to piping through \fBpv\fR.
.TP
.B \-V
SHA-256 end-to-end file verification. After the sender finishes,
a SHA-256 hash of all transmitted data is sent and verified by
the receiver. Both sides must use \fB\-V\fR.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Set custom chat nickname. Displayed to the peer instead of
Server/Client. The nickname is exchanged encrypted after ECDHE
handshake. Used in chat mode (\fB\-c\fR).
.TP
.B \-u
UDP mode. Use datagrams instead of TCP stream. ECDHE handshake
is performed over UDP before encrypted communication begins.
.TP
.B \-4
Force IPv4 only.
.TP
.B \-6
Force IPv6 only.
.TP
.B \-c
Chat mode. Display messages with timestamps and colored roles
(green for local, cyan for remote).
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose output. Use \fB\-vv\fR for extra debug information.
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " file"
Read the password from the first line of \fIfile\fR (should be mode 0600).
Without \fB\-k\fR or \fB\-\-key\-file\fR, the password is taken from the
\fBCLAWSEC_PASSWORD\fR environment variable (removed from the environment
after reading), or prompted for on the terminal without echo.
\fB\-k\fR is visible to other users until ClawSec overwrites it.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-clients " n"
With \fB\-K\fR: at most \fIn\fR concurrent handlers (default 16); further
clients wait in the listen queue.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-per\-ip " n"
With \fB\-K\fR: at most \fIn\fR concurrent handlers per client address
(IPv6: per /64 network); further connections from that address are closed
immediately (default 8, 0 = unlimited).
.TP
.BI \-\-users " file"
Server: per-user passwords instead of one shared password. Each line of
\fIfile\fR is \fIname\fR:v1:\fIhex\fR, the user's SPAKE2 password scalar
(Argon2id of the password with a per-user salt), written by
\fB\-\-add\-user\fR. The file is read for every connection, so users can
be added or removed without a restart. Unknown users fail exactly like wrong
passwords. Keep the file private (mode 0600): a line lets its holder log in
as that user. Not available with \fB\-u\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-add\-user " name"
With \fB\-\-users\fR: add \fIname\fR or change its password (asked twice on
the terminal, or taken from \fB\-\-key\-file\fR / \fBCLAWSEC_PASSWORD\fR), then exit.
Names: 1\-32 characters from A\-Z a\-z 0\-9 . _ \-.
.TP
.BI \-\-user " name"
Client: log in as \fIname\fR on a server that runs \fB\-\-users\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth\-fail\-limit " n"
With \fB\-K\fR: after \fIn\fR failed password attempts from one address
within 10 minutes, refuse that address for 10 minutes (default 5,
0 = off). Blocks are always logged.
.TP
.BI \-\-handshake\-timeout " seconds"
Abort session setup (TLS camouflage, knock, handshake)
that takes longer than \fIseconds\fR (default 15, 0 = no limit).
.TP
.BI \-\-socks\-allow " list"
SOCKS server: allow only destinations inside the comma-separated
address/CIDR \fIlist\fR. Default: everything except loopback, link-local
(cloud metadata), multicast and reserved ranges. Checked on resolved
addresses.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the ClawSec release and wire protocol version, then exit.
.TP
.BI \-w " secs"
Connection timeout in seconds.
.TP
.BI \-e " program"
Execute program for each connection (encrypted reverse shell).
Not built by default; build with
\fBmake linux DFLAGS=\-DGAPING_SECURITY_HOLE\fR. Anyone who knows the
password gets the program.
.TP
.B \-h
Display usage information.
.SH MODES
.SS Chat Mode
Interactive encrypted chat with timestamps:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 8888 \-k "pass" \-c
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-c host 8888
.fi
.SS File Transfer
Pipe files through encrypted connection:
.PP
.nf
Receive: clawsec \-v \-l \-p 8888 \-k "pass" > file
Send:    clawsec \-v \-k "pass" host 8888 < file
.fi
.SS Reverse Shell
Encrypted remote command execution:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 8888 \-k "pass" \-e /bin/bash
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" host 8888
.fi
.SS Keep-Open (Multi-Client)
Accept multiple clients on one port:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-K \-p 8888 \-k "pass"
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" host 8888
.fi
.SS Port Forwarding
Encrypted tunnel to internal service (replaces ssh \-L):
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 8888 \-k "pass" \-L 127.0.0.1:3306
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" server 8888
.fi
.SS Traffic Obfuscation
Hide encrypted traffic as HTTP requests:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-obfs http
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-obfs http host 443
.fi
.SS Encrypted Client Hello (ECH)
Add a GREASE Encrypted Client Hello extension (the SNI stays visible):
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-obfs tls
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-ech host 443
.fi
.SS Multiplexed Tunnel
Multiple connections over one encrypted link:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 4430 \-k "pass" \-L internal:80 \-\-mux
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-p 8080 \-\-mux server 4430
.fi
.SS Fallback
Proxy clients without the knock to another service:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-fallback nginx.local:80
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-fallback nginx.local:80 server 443
.fi
.PP
When curl or a browser connects to port 443, they see the real nginx site.
Only ClawSec clients get the encrypted tunnel.
.SS TLS Fingerprinting
Disguise traffic as a specific browser:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-fallback nginx:80
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-fingerprint chrome \-\-fallback nginx:80 server 443
.fi
.PP
Observers see a Chrome-like (not identical) TLS ClientHello.
.SS TOFU (Trust On First Use)
SSH-like server identity verification:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-tofu
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-tofu server 443
.fi
.PP
First connection saves the server fingerprint. Subsequent connections
verify it. If the server key changes, the connection is aborted.
.SS Post-Quantum Hybrid Key Exchange
Quantum-resistant key exchange with ML-KEM-768:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-pq
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-pq server 443
.fi
.PP
Combine with TOFU for maximum security:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-pq \-\-tofu
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-pq \-\-tofu server 443
.fi
.SS TUN VPN
Zero-config encrypted VPN (requires root/sudo):
.PP
.nf
Server: sudo clawsec \-l \-k "pass" \-p 9000 \-\-tun 10.0.0.1/24 \-\-masquerade
Client: sudo clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-tun 10.0.0.2/24 \-\-default\-route server 9000
.fi
.PP
With UDP data channel (avoids TCP-over-TCP meltdown):
.PP
.nf
Server: sudo clawsec \-l \-k "pass" \-p 9000 \-\-tun 10.0.0.1/24 \-\-masquerade \-\-tun\-udp
Client: sudo clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-tun 10.0.0.2/24 \-\-default\-route \-\-tun\-udp server 9000
.fi
.SS SOCKS5 Proxy
Route any application through encrypted tunnel:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 9000 \-k "pass" \-\-socks 0
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-socks 1080 server 9000
curl \-\-proxy socks5://127.0.0.1:1080 https://ifconfig.me
.fi
.SS Reverse Tunnel
SSH-like reverse port forwarding:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-p 9000 \-k "pass" \-R 0.0.0.0:8080
Client: clawsec \-k "pass" \-R 127.0.0.1:3000 server 9000
.fi
.SS Encrypted File Transfer
Dedicated file transfer with SHA-256 verification:
.PP
.nf
Recv:   clawsec \-l \-p 4444 \-k "pass" \-\-recv ./incoming
Send:   clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-send backup.tar.gz server 4444
.fi
.SS Port Scanner
Port scanning with banner grabbing:
.PP
.nf
clawsec \-\-scan 1\-1024 \-b target.com
.fi
.SS Compressed Transfer with Verification
Transfer files with zlib compression, progress, and SHA-256 verification:
.PP
.nf
Send:   clawsec \-z \-P \-V \-k "pass" server 9999 < bigfile.tar
Recv:   clawsec \-l \-p 9999 \-z \-P \-V \-k "pass" > bigfile.tar
.fi
.SS UDP Mode
Encrypted UDP datagrams:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-u \-p 9000 \-k "pass"
Client: echo "data" | clawsec \-u \-k "pass" host 9000
.fi
.SS IPv6
Force IPv6 transport:
.PP
.nf
Server: clawsec \-l \-6 \-p 8888 \-k "pass"
Client: clawsec \-6 \-k "pass" ::1 8888
.fi
.SH SECURITY
.SS Cryptography
.IP \(bu 2
SPAKE2 password-authenticated key exchange (RFC 9382, P-256) with
transcript-bound mutual key confirmation
.IP \(bu 2
Argon2id (libargon2, t=3, m=19 MiB, p=1) for the password scalar
.IP \(bu 2
AES-256-GCM (AEAD) records with authenticated headers
.IP \(bu 2
HKDF-SHA256 directional traffic keys (client\->server, server\->client; TCP and UDP)
.IP \(bu 2
Optional ML-KEM-768 hybrid (\fB\-\-pq\fR) and Ed25519 server identity (\fB\-\-tofu\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
Strict sequence numbers (replay and reflection protection)
.SS Handshake Protocol (wire protocol v4)
.PP
.nf
1. S\->C  hello ("CS", version 4, option flags), SPAKE2 pB
          [, identity key] [, ML\-KEM public key]
2. C\->S  hello, SPAKE2 pA [, ML\-KEM ciphertext]
3. S\->C  confirmation cB [, Ed25519 signature over the transcript]
4. C\->S  confirmation cA
Session key = HKDF(SPAKE2 Ke [|| ML\-KEM secret], transcript hash)
.fi
.PP
Another protocol version or different \fB\-\-tofu\fR/\fB\-\-pq\fR settings are
reported at the hello; a wrong password or a modified handshake fails the
confirmation on both ends.
.SS Wire Format
.PP
.nf
[MAGIC:4][VERSION:2][FLAGS:2][SEQ:4][LENGTH:4][IV:12][TAG:16][CIPHERTEXT]
.fi
.PP
VERSION is 4. The 16-byte header is authenticated as GCM associated data.
Protocol v4 (ClawSec 4.x) is not compatible with ClawSec 3.x and earlier.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAWSEC_PASSWORD
Session password when neither \fB\-k\fR nor \fB\-\-key\-file\fR is given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Error (connection failed, authentication failed, etc.)
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Encrypted chat between two machines:
.PP
.nf
$ clawsec \-l \-p 9999 \-k "MyS3cur3Pass!" \-c
$ clawsec \-k "MyS3cur3Pass!" \-c 192.168.1.10 9999
.fi
.PP
Transfer a directory:
.PP
.nf
$ tar czf \- /data | clawsec \-k "pass" host 9999
$ clawsec \-l \-p 9999 \-k "pass" | tar xzf \-
.fi
.PP
All traffic-shaping options (Chrome-like ClientHello, fallback service):
.PP
.nf
Server:  clawsec \-l \-p 443 \-k "pass" \-\-fallback 127.0.0.1:80 \-\-ech \-\-pad
Client:  clawsec \-k "pass" \-\-fingerprint chrome \-\-fallback 127.0.0.1:80 \-\-ech \-\-pad server 443
.fi
.PP
Note: \fB\-\-fingerprint\fR is client-side only (shapes outgoing ClientHello).
